<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sipontum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sipontum was an ancient port on the Gulf of Manfredonia in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, said in legend to have been founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> planted a colony there in 194 BCE, and it later became an early Christian bishopric whose basilica of Santa Maria di Siponto survives. Earthquakes and malaria led King Manfred to move its people to his new town of Manfredonia in the 1250s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sipontum.jpg" alt="Sipontum" width="320" height="140" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salvatore Triventi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PiazzaSiponto3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
